  "prompts": [
    {
      "id": "scope",
      "text": "First, put one question inside a Warp: an isolated, reversible piece of work. It is a project boundary, not a mathematical force. We test the animation before building its explanation.",
      "taskId": "c878bdbc-05bc-4a4c-9b4f-827aeb386926"
    },
    {
      "id": "origin",
      "text": "Your idea gave each point its own origin. Here we shift zeta's output origin smoothly from zero toward one. That turns two separate pictures into one changing equation.",
      "taskId": "47081731-5d87-4193-a55f-8653f21e8daf"
    },
    {
      "id": "prior_work",
      "text": "Wegert's phase plots explain the color view. Lester's work places zeta's a-points in an established research area. We connect those ideas to our earlier two-state switch, using classical prediction and Newton correction.",
      "taskId": "f9b82a90-1fa9-4757-812a-f8cae76836fa"
    },
    {
      "id": "prior_results",
      "text": "Platt and Trudgian provide rigorous finite checks. Lamzouri's preprint presents a proportion bound; OpenAI reports a zero-free region and formalization. These concern zeta zeros, not a certificate for our moving nonzero targets.",
      "taskId": "728911ac-3ea4-488d-9195-679a78873c6c"
    },
    {
      "id": "tracking",
      "text": "We follow the same root as the target moves. Where zeta prime is nonzero, this formula gives its direction and speed. Smaller steps and corrections help the computation stay on track.",
      "taskId": "762aa101-5c09-4fd4-b3ba-897d29628d05"
    },
    {
      "id": "results",
      "text": "Independent checks find two paths leaving the right edge before the target reaches one. The third reaches a finite one-point. Leaving our window means the view ended, not that the mathematical branch stopped.",
      "taskId": "883e969b-1532-4c93-85dc-803c2e95cc02"
    },
    {
      "id": "combine",
      "text": "Color shows phase. Continuation follows a solution. Higher precision checks numerical stability. Analytic estimates would be needed for a certificate. These methods help separate tracked connections from artifacts of switching or coarse pixels.",
      "taskId": "b631bfb8-6560-4b55-ab6c-d57bc59b7596"
    },
    {
      "id": "conjecture",
      "text": "Our conjecture required all three paths to remain inside the window. The computed exits give numerical counterevidence. A rigorous rejection would require certified continuation bounds. We keep the original window.",
      "taskId": "399e60fb-1591-4bb6-80e0-954ef9db6dfe"
    },
    {
      "id": "rh_gap",
      "text": "One-points are not zeta zeros. Even three successful paths would not exclude an unseen zero off the critical line. A proof of Riemann needs a global constraint, beyond what these pictures establish.",
      "taskId": "79d3a765-47d3-42d6-a925-a04bfeb60ed1"
    }
  ]
